<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">State of Yan</h1><p class="st-meta">1046 BCE – 222 BCE (approx.) · capital Ji (Jicheng)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The northernmost of the great Zhou states, centred on Beijing, which nearly destroyed Qi in 284 BCE and sent the assassin Jing Ke against the King of Qin.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Yan was granted after the Zhou conquest to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-of-shao">Duke of Shao</a>, a senior royal kinsman, whose son took up the fief in the far north. Its capital Ji stood in the area of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>; the early Yan cemetery at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liulihe">Liulihe</a> has yielded inscribed bronzes confirming the founding.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Isolated on the frontier with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/donghu">Donghu</a> and other northern peoples, Yan stayed on the margins for centuries before becoming one of the seven <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States</a>. A disastrous abdication by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-kuai-of-yan">Kuai</a> in 316 BCE provoked civil war and a Qi invasion. King Zhao rebuilt the state, recruited the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yue-yi">Yue Yi</a> and in 284 BCE captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linzi">Linzi</a>. Yan built long walls in the north, forerunners of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-wall-of-china">Great Wall</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Crown Prince Dan's attempt to have <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jing-ke">Jing Ke</a> assassinate the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> in 227 BCE provoked a Qin onslaught. Ji fell in 226 BCE and King Xi fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a>, where he was captured in 222 BCE. The name Yan remains a literary name for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/state-of-yan.jpg" alt="State of Yan" width="320" height="295"><figcaption>Image: Philg88 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EN-YAN260BCE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1046 BCE – 222 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century-bce">11th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ji (Jicheng)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>King Zhao of Yan, King Xi of Yan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Zhou ancestral religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Yan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-634&amp;focus=state-of-yan" aria-label="State of Yan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7547 750 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where State of Yan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7867 1006L7768 1193M7867 1006L7855 1179M7867 1006L7768 1187"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7768 1193h0M7855 1179h0M7768 1187h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7867 1006h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7867 1006h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Ji. <a href="/atlas?year=-634&amp;focus=state-of-yan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

